WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.503
Lesson seven.

00:00:01.503 --> 00:00:03.041
Reading the weights.

00:00:03.041 --> 00:00:07.380
Because the pilot is linear, we can understand exactly what it learned.

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:09.218
Let's take it apart.

00:00:09.818 --> 00:00:11.665
Start with one decision.

00:00:11.665 --> 00:00:14.379
The lander is high up and falling fast.

00:00:14.379 --> 00:00:19.517
Each input is multiplied by its weight, and the products are added up per action.

00:00:19.517 --> 00:00:25.463
The falling speed alone gives the main engine a big push, and takes a lot away from do nothing.

00:00:25.463 --> 00:00:27.437
The main engine wins.

00:00:28.037 --> 00:00:31.805
A few frames earlier, the lander was tilted to the right.

00:00:31.805 --> 00:00:33.139
Now it's close.

00:00:33.139 --> 00:00:38.472
The tilt and the height add votes to the left engine, and it wins by a small margin.

00:00:38.472 --> 00:00:41.555
Firing it turns the lander back upright.

00:00:42.155 --> 00:00:45.564
Near the ground, the lander is spinning to the left.

00:00:45.564 --> 00:00:50.905
Spin has the biggest weight of all, on the left engine, and here it counts against it.

00:00:50.905 --> 00:00:54.768
The left engine drops far behind, and the right engine wins.

00:00:54.768 --> 00:00:57.057
That spins the lander back.

00:00:57.657 --> 00:00:58.859
But be careful.

00:00:58.859 --> 00:01:01.680
A big weight doesn't always mean a big effect.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:04.383
Here are the sizes of all the weights.

00:01:04.983 --> 00:01:09.551
What also matters is how much each input actually changes in flight.

00:01:09.551 --> 00:01:11.294
Altitude moves a lot.

00:01:11.294 --> 00:01:16.523
Spin barely moves at all, so its large weights are multiplied by small numbers.

00:01:16.523 --> 00:01:19.467
The legs are always zero in the air.

00:01:20.067 --> 00:01:24.007
Multiply the two, and you get the influence of each weight.

00:01:24.007 --> 00:01:31.365
The three that matter most are drift on the left engine, and falling speed on do nothing and on the main engine.

00:01:31.965 --> 00:01:34.390
We can also draw a rule as a map.

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:40.542
Across is the tilt, and up and down is the spin, with every other input held at a typical value.

00:01:40.542 --> 00:01:44.569
Each colour is the action the pilot would choose there.

00:01:45.169 --> 00:01:47.547
The white line is a real flight.

00:01:47.547 --> 00:01:54.742
It stays near the middle, where the colours meet, so the pilot hops between engines with every small change.

00:01:55.342 --> 00:01:56.974
Here's a second map.

00:01:56.974 --> 00:02:01.180
Across is the height, and up and down is the falling speed.

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:04.492
Falling fast, it fires the main engine.

00:02:04.492 --> 00:02:08.473
Falling slowly while still high up, it uses the side engines.

00:02:08.473 --> 00:02:12.165
And close to the ground, moving slowly, it does nothing.

00:02:12.165 --> 00:02:15.050
The real flight zig-zags along the border.

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:19.504
The pilot brakes just enough, again and again, all the way down.

00:02:20.104 --> 00:02:23.445
Now let's put it together, one action at a time.

00:02:23.445 --> 00:02:26.847
The main engine fires when the lander is falling.

00:02:26.847 --> 00:02:31.741
Its bias is negative, so it stays off unless the fall is fast enough.

00:02:32.341 --> 00:02:39.418
Do nothing gets a strong vote when the lander is rising, and a strong vote against it when the left leg is down.

00:02:39.418 --> 00:02:42.254
This pilot almost never just waits.

00:02:42.854 --> 00:02:45.718
The left engine fixes drift and spin.

00:02:45.718 --> 00:02:52.192
It fires when the lander drifts right, or spins clockwise, and it pushes the lander back.

00:02:52.792 --> 00:02:55.376
And the right engine is the default.

00:02:55.376 --> 00:03:00.720
It fires almost half the time, in small, cheap pulses, whenever no other rule wins.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.187
There's no rule for drifting left.

00:03:03.187 --> 00:03:09.183
When that happens, the left engine loses its votes, and the right engine wins by default.

00:03:09.783 --> 00:03:13.554
Finally, let's test which weights the pilot really needs.

00:03:13.554 --> 00:03:18.428
We set one weight to zero, fly thirty episodes, and see how many still land.

00:03:18.428 --> 00:03:21.629
Then we put it back, and try the next one.

00:03:22.229 --> 00:03:24.805
Most weights can go with little harm.

00:03:24.805 --> 00:03:30.701
But remove the rule that links falling speed to the main engine, and not a single flight lands.

00:03:30.701 --> 00:03:34.493
Without it, the pilot never brakes, and simply falls.
